{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-182715",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to express the summation $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k).$\n\nLet ${a}_{i}$ for $i\\ge 0$ be a sequence, and let $S(k)$ denote the sum of the first $k+1$ terms.\n\n$$\nS(k)={\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{a}_{i}\n$$\n\nWe can always express $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k)$ by writing the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${a}_{i}=2-i.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1}=2-(k+1)=1-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}(2-i)=({\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}(2-i))+(1-k),\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as \n\n$$\nS(k+1)=S(k)+1-k.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
